Item 04 is solved: the gate passes, I've written the PROGRESS.md entry, and the item is marked `[x]` in iterations.md. I haven't committed or pushed.

**How the engine is organised.** It is one module, `racket/engine.rkt`, that `include`s the ported files (`racket/engine/*.rktl`) in the same load order as `metacat.ss`. The original's files call each other in both directions and `set!` each other's globals. Racket doesn't allow module cycles or `set!` on imported variables, so one module is the closest match to Chez loading everything into a single global scope. Each `.rktl` stays a line-for-line copy of its `.ss` file. The full reasoning is in `docs/porting-notes.md`. Three pieces support this:
- **`racket/engine-lang.rkt`**: a small module language that throws away the values of top-level expressions, as Chez does. Plain `racket/base` would print every `'done`.
- **`racket/engine/pending.rktl`**: placeholders for names from files not ported yet, grouped by original file. Each later item deletes its names there; a leftover name becomes a duplicate definition, which Racket refuses to compile.
- **`set-global!`**: the way tests, and later the CLI and GUI, set engine globals, since code outside a module can't `set!` its variables.

**What was ported:**
- **`constants.ss`**: only the probability distributions. Colours, fonts and window sizes wait for the GUI items.
- **`setup.ss`**: the globals and user commands. `setup` and `enable-resizing` create windows, so they move to the GUI layer.
- **`coderack.ss`**: unchanged except that the codelet-type list uses the module-level form of its macro.
- **`descriptions.ss`**: unchanged.
- **`compat.rkt`**: gained Chez's `case`, which accepts a bare symbol as a clause key; `coderack.ss` relies on it.

**Tests:**
- **The coderack check** (`tests/diff/coderack-battery.scm`, 43 tests): the same tests run under Chez with the original loaded and under Racket with the engine, and the outputs must match line for line. They cover bins, urgencies, posting, deletion, deferred posting, clamping, and codelet selection across 8 seeds × 7 temperatures, with the random-number state recorded after every choice. Stand-in objects play the Workspace, Themespace, Trace and Slipnet. All 43 match.
- **Written first:** I wrote the battery and ran it under Chez before any engine code existed. The first Racket runs failed because of two problems on the test side, not in the ported model code:
  - the test namespace was loading its own copy of the engine;
  - one test mapped over a literal list, which Chez's compiler handles in its own order.
- **The check catches real breakage:** I made five deliberate changes to `coderack.rktl`, one at a time. Each made between 1 and 17 tests fail, and the restored file matches again.
- **Engine structure** (`racket/tests/engine-test.rkt`, written after the code): loading prints nothing, `set-global!` works, and loading the engine pulls in no `racket/gui` or `racket/draw`.

**Limits:**
- **Description codelets are untested.** Making a description needs the Workspace, Slipnet and formulas, none of which are ported yet. Only `descriptions-equal?` and `description-member?` are tested now. The codelets will be checked by the golden traces once those files exist.
- **The headless driver has a requirement.** As in the oracle, it must give every codelet type an empty Coderack window, or running a codelet fails.

I also reorganised the test plumbing:
- The shared battery helpers moved to `tests/diff/helpers.scm`.
- `diff-eval.ss` now takes several files and defines `b:set-global!`.
- Both differential tests now share one Racket runner, `racket/tests/diff-runner.rkt`.

